  Top-level Structure

  - episodes: List of 155,098 navigation episodes (largest dataset so far)
  - instruction_vocab: Empty vocabulary structure (all fields are empty/None)

  Individual Episode Structure

  Each episode contains the same basic structure as R2R and EnvDrop, but with some key differences:

  - episode_id: Unique identifier (integer, starts from 0)
  - trajectory_id: Complex ID string with pattern scalevln_{scene_id}__fake_{start}_{end}
  - scene_id: HM3D scene identifier (format: "hm3d/{scene_id}/{scene_id}.basis.glb")
  - start_position: Starting coordinates [x, y, z] (list of floats)
  - start_rotation: Starting orientation [x, y, z, w] (list of floats)
  - goals: Target position with radius (list of dict with 'position' and 'radius')
  - info: Always contains None for geodesic_distance
  - instruction: Navigation instruction containing:
    - instruction_text: Natural language instruction string (present)
    - instruction_tokens: Always None (not tokenized)
  - reference_path: Optimal path coordinates (list of [x, y, z] positions)

  Key Differences from Other Datasets

  1. Missing Data:
    - No geodesic_distance values (all None)
    - No tokenized instructions (all None)
    - Empty vocabulary structure
  2. Scene Type: Uses HM3D (Habitat-Matterport 3D) scenes instead of MP3D
  3. Trajectory ID Pattern: Complex naming with scalevln_ prefix and __fake_ suffix
  4. Goal Radius: Consistently 0.25 meters (much smaller than R2R/EnvDrop's 3.0m)
  5. Scale: 155,098 episodes (largest dataset examined)

  Sample Data

  - Instruction: "turn around and walk out of the bedroom . turn right and walk down the stairs . stop
   at the bottom of the stairs ."
  - Start position: [6.18, 0.07, 1.75]
  - Goal position: [-0.08, -2.53, 0.07] (significant elevation change)
  - Success radius: 0.25 meters (much more precise)
  - Reference path: 7 waypoints showing multi-level navigation

  Dataset Characteristics

  - Scene format: Uses .basis.glb files (compressed 3D format)
  - Navigation complexity: Multi-level navigation with stairs
  - Trajectory naming: Suggests synthetic or augmented data ("fake" in IDs)
  - Precision: Much smaller success radius indicates more precise navigation tasks

